  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Let $x\\in R$ and $y\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $∃\\,y\\in R,\\,5y=1.$\n\nII. The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,xy=1\\,)$ is $R.$\n\nIII. $∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃y\\in R,\\,xy=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃y\\in R,\\,xy=1\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For every real $x$ there exists real $y$ such that $xy=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $5y=1$ for some real number $y$ (e.g., for  $y=\\frac{1}{5}$).\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∃\\,y\\in R,\\,xy=1\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(x)$ is true for some $x\\in R$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,y\\in R,\\,1\\cdot y=1\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $y=1.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,∃\\,y\\in R,\\,0\\cdot y=1\n$$\n\nis false.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃y\\in R,\\,xy=1\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀\\,x\\in R\\,(\\,{{∃y\\in R,\\,xy=1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $R.$ And, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
